    {
      "text": "I was able to execute the request to retrieve the text using the ISBN `978-0-26203-847-1`.\n\nHowever, the retrieval tool only returned the beginning of **Chapter 1**. It did not return the full text of Chapters 1 through 4, nor did it return the entirety of Chapter 1, as the returned text segment ends mid-paragraph.\n\nSince the tool only provided a partial view of Chapter 1 via the `fetch_by_identifier` call and I cannot request specific chapters or pages through that function, I cannot fulfill your request to read Chapters 1 through 4 at this time. I can only provide the partial text that was retrieved:\n\n***\n\n**Partial Text Retrieved for Chapter 1: The Determinism Problem in Policy Analysis**\n\n> Public policy is made for a future that cannot be known. This is not a new observation — it is embedded in every cost-benefit framework, every regulatory impact assessment, every scenario planning exercise conducted since the postwar formalization of policy analysis as a discipline. And yet the dominant tools of policy design remain stubbornly deterministic. They produce point estimates, project single futures, and commit resources to courses of action on the assumption that the model's central scenario will materialize. When it does not — as it routinely does not — the response is typically to update the model and commit again.\n>\n> This book argues that the problem is structural, not technical. Deterministic policy design is not merely a consequence of computational limitations or data poverty; it is the expression of an underlying epistemic stance that treats uncertainty as a temporary condition to be resolved rather than a permanent feature to be managed. We propose an alternative stance, and with it an alternative set of tools: a probabilistic approach to policy design in which uncertainty is not a residual but a primary variable, and in which the goal of policy analysis is not to eliminate uncertainty but to produce commitments that remain robust across the range of futures that uncertainty permits.\n>\n> The framework we develop — which we call adaptive policy envelope design, or APED — draws on three intellectual traditions. The first is Bayesian decision theory, particularly the work on decision-making under deep uncertainty developed in the 1990s and 2000s. The second is robust optimization, especially the scenario-based methods developed for engineering design under parametric uncertainty. The third is adaptive management theory from environmental governance, which has for three decades grappled with the challenge of making irreversible resource commitments in systems whose dynamics are not fully understood.\n\n***\n\nI will now search the web again to see if any online source explicitly lists the contents of Chapters 2, 3, and 4, which you need for the rest of your evaluation.",
